\section{Enabling Full-Duplex Communication}

\subsection*{(a) Conceptual Approach}
Employ an event-driven loop (\texttt{select()}, \texttt{poll()}, or
\texttt{epoll()}) that watches \emph{both} the data socket and
\texttt{stdin}. Whenever \texttt{select()} signals readiness on either
descriptor, process that side immediately while the other remains
non-blocking.  This yields simultaneous send/receive capability (full duplex).

\subsection*{(b) Outline of Code Changes (no code yet)}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Create a permanent \texttt{select()} loop in both programs.
  \item Each iteration, build the descriptor sets:
        \begin{itemize}
          \item Add the data socket to \lstinline|readmask| (and optionally
                \lstinline|exceptmask|).
          \item Add \lstinline|STDIN_FILENO| (\texttt{0}) to \lstinline|readmask|.
        \end{itemize}
  \item After \texttt{select()} returns:
        \begin{itemize}
          \item If the socket is readable $\rightarrow$ \lstinline|recv()| and
                process/display the data.
          \item If \texttt{stdin} is readable $\rightarrow$ read the line and
                \lstinline|send()| it immediately.
        \end{itemize}
  \item Set the socket non-blocking (or handle \texttt{EAGAIN}/\texttt{EWOULDBLOCK})
        so one stalled I/O path never delays the other.
  \item Graceful shutdown: when either side sends the termination token
        (\texttt{"."}), close the socket and exit the loop.
  \item Remove the existing prompt-then-wait half-duplex logic; the unified
        \texttt{select()} loop supersedes it.
\end{enumerate}

